      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "Hello~ I didn't modify the model architecture to better fit NLP tasks, as I  thought that any structural changes might inadvertently degrade the performance. I simply reshaped the wikitext dataset to match the exact data format used in the Sudoku task in this project adapting the fixed 81-length input/output sequences accordingly. So, this is just aligning sequence dimension to the HRM time dimension instead of predicting in blocks. Thus, as I understand, the HRM could be overfitting all the time with this method to learn from wikitext but evaluation on the test set showed an unexpectedly good performance, which still puzzles me. Here is the code that I convert the wikitext to HRM Sudoku format: https://github.com/johnnyZeppelin/HRM-Idioma\n\n> Hi, how are you structuring the input/output? Are you aligning sequence dimension to the HRM time dimension, or predicting in blocks? Curious to see your approach here.\n\n",
